Below is a compact, self‑contained C++ implementation that returns **all** permutations of a given string.  
It uses a classic back‑tracking routine (recursive swap) – this is the most common way to generate permutations in interview questions and gives you full control over the order in which the permutations are produced.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>   // for std::swap

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Generate all permutations of a string
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
std::vector<std::string> permute(const std::string& s)
{
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    if (s.empty()) return result;          // nothing to permute

    // Work on a mutable copy of the string
    std::string cur = s;
    std::vector<int> idx(cur.size(), 0);   // helper for recursion

    // Recursive lambda – captures result by reference
    std::function<void(int)> backtrack = [&](int pos)
    {
        if (pos == static_cast<int>(cur.size())) {
            result.push_back(cur);        // a full permutation
            return;
        }

        for (int i = pos; i < static_cast<int>(cur.size()); ++i) {
            std::swap(cur[pos], cur[i]);  // put i‑th char at position pos
            backtrack(pos + 1);           // recurse for the rest
            std::swap(cur[pos], cur[i]);  // backtrack – restore original order
        }
    };

    backtrack(0);
    return result;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Demo / test
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
int main()
{
    std::string str = "abc";
    std::vector<std::string> perms = permute(str);

    std::cout << "Permutations of \"" << str << "\":\n";
    for (const auto& p : perms) {
        std::cout << p << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

1. **Mutable copy** – We copy the input string into `cur` so we can swap characters in place.
2. **Backtracking** –  
   * `pos` is the index of the character we are currently fixing.  
   * For every position `i` from `pos` to the end, we swap `cur[pos]` with `cur[i]`, recurse to fill the rest of the string (`pos+1`), then swap back to restore the original order.
3. **Base case** – When `pos` reaches the string length, we have a complete permutation, which we push into `result`.

### Complexity

*Time*: `O(n! * n)` – we generate `n!` permutations and each permutation is built in `O(n)` time (copying the string into the result vector).  
*Space*: `O(n! * n)` for the output vector plus `O(n)` for the recursion stack and the mutable string.

### Alternative: `std::next_permutation`

If you only need the permutations in lexicographic order and don’t care about the recursion, you can use the STL algorithm:

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> permuteLexicographic(std::string s)
{
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    std::sort(s.begin(), s.end());          // start from the smallest permutation
    do {
        result.push_back(s);
    } while (std::next_permutation(s.begin(), s.end()));
    return result;
}
```

Both approaches give you the same set of permutations; choose the one that best fits your needs.

Here are the two most common ways to generate permutations in C++.

### Method 1: Using `std::next_permutation` (Recommended / Idiomatic)
This is the **standard C++ way**. It is efficient, handles duplicates automatically (generates unique permutations), and requires minimal code.

**Requirement:** The string must be sorted initially to generate *all* permutations.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm> // for std::sort, std::next_permutation
#include <vector>

std::vector<std::string> getPermutations(std::string s) {
    std::vector<std::string> result;

    // 1. Sort the string to start from the lexicographically smallest permutation
    std::sort(s.begin(), s.end());

    // 2. Generate permutations until next_permutation returns false
    do {
        result.push_back(s);
    } while (std::next_permutation(s.begin(), s.end()));

    return result;
}
```

