### Method 2: Recursive Backtracking (Classic Algorithm)
This is the standard "textbook" implementation. It is useful for understanding the logic or if you cannot use the STL for some reason. 

**Note:** This generates duplicates if the input string has repeated characters (e.g., "AAB" will generate 6 entries instead of 3 unique ones). See the "Handling Duplicates" section below for a fix.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

void permuteRecursive(std::string& s, int left, int right, std::vector<std::string>& result) {
    // Base case: If left == right, we have a fixed permutation
    if (left == right) {
        result.push_back(s);
        return;
    }

    // Recursive case: Swap each character into the 'left' position
    for (int i = left; i <= right; ++i) {
        // 1. Choose: Swap current index with left index
        std::swap(s[left], s[i]);

        // 2. Explore: Recurse for the next position
        permuteRecursive(s, left + 1, right, result);

        // 3. Un-choose (Backtrack): Swap back to restore original string for next iteration
        std::swap(s[left], s[i]);
    }
}

std::vector<std::string> getPermutationsRecursive(std::string s) {
    std::vector<std::string> result;
    if (!s.empty()) {
        permuteRecursive(s, 0, s.length() - 1, result);
    }
    return result;
}
```

---

### Handling Duplicate Characters (Recursive Version)
If your input string has duplicate characters (e.g., `"AAB"`), the recursive method above produces duplicate permutations. To fix this, use a `std::unordered_set` (or `std::set`) at each recursion level to track which characters have already been placed at the current `left` index.

```cpp
#include <unordered_set>

void permuteUniqueRecursive(std::string& s, int left, int right, std::vector<std::string>& result) {
    if (left == right) {
        result.push_back(s);
        return;
    }

    // Track characters used at this specific recursion depth (index 'left')
    std::unordered_set<char> used; 

    for (int i = left; i <= right; ++i) {
        // If we have already placed this character at index 'left', skip it
        if (used.count(s[i])) continue;

        used.insert(s[i]); // Mark as used for this position

        std::swap(s[left], s[i]);
        permuteUniqueRecursive(s, left + 1, right, result);
        std::swap(s[left], s[i]); // Backtrack
    }
}
```
*(Note: `std::next_permutation` in Method 1 handles this automatically without extra code.)*

