### Complete Runnable Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

// --- Method 1: STL (Best for production) ---
std::vector<std::string> getPermutationsSTL(std::string s) {
    std::vector<std::string> res;
    std::sort(s.begin(), s.end());
    do { res.push_back(s); } while (std::next_permutation(s.begin(), s.end()));
    return res;
}

// --- Method 2: Recursive Backtracking (Educational) ---
void backtrack(std::string& s, int l, int r, std::vector<std::string>& res) {
    if (l == r) { res.push_back(s); return; }
    for (int i = l; i <= r; ++i) {
        std::swap(s[l], s[i]);
        backtrack(s, l + 1, r, res);
        std::swap(s[l], s[i]);
    }
}
std::vector<std::string> getPermutationsRec(std::string s) {
    std::vector<std::string> res;
    if (!s.empty()) backtrack(s, 0, s.size() - 1, res);
    return res;
}

int main() {
    std::string input = "ABC";

    std::cout << "Input: " << input << "\n\n";

    // Using STL
    std::cout << "--- STL Method (Sorted, Unique) ---\n";
    auto perms1 = getPermutationsSTL(input);
    for (const auto& p : perms1) std::cout << p << ' ';
    std::cout << "\nCount: " << perms1.size() << "\n\n";

    // Using Recursion
    std::cout << "--- Recursive Method ---\n";
    auto perms2 = getPermutationsRec(input);
    for (const auto& p : perms2) std::cout << p << ' ';
    std::cout << "\nCount: " << perms2.size() << "\n";

    // Test with duplicates
    std::string dupInput = "AAB";
    std::cout << "\n--- Duplicate Test: " << dupInput << " ---\n";
    std::cout << "STL (Unique): ";
    for (auto& p : getPermutationsSTL(dupInput)) std::cout << p << ' ';
    std::cout << "\nRecursive (Has Duplicates): ";
    for (auto& p : getPermutationsRec(dupInput)) std::cout << p << ' ';
    std::cout << "\n";

    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis

| Method | Time Complexity | Space Complexity | Notes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`std::next_permutation`** | **O(N! * N)** | **O(N! * N)** (to store results) / **O(1)** auxiliary | Generates in **lexicographical order**. Handles duplicates natively. Fastest in practice due to compiler optimizations. |
| **Recursive Backtracking** | **O(N! * N)** | **O(N! * N)** (results) + **O(N)** (call stack) | Order depends on swap logic (usually not sorted). Easy to modify for specific constraints (e.g., "generate permutations of length K"). |

### Which one should you use?
1.  **Use `std::next_permutation` (Method 1)** for almost all real-world C++ applications. It is standard, optimized, bug-free, and handles duplicates correctly.
2.  **Use Recursive Backtracking (Method 2)** only for coding interviews (if asked to implement it manually), competitive programming with specific custom constraints, or educational purposes.

Here is an efficient and standard C++ solution to generate all permutations of a string using the **Backtracking** algorithm. This approach is preferred because it avoids creating excessive temporary string objects and runs in $O(N \times N!)$ time complexity.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm> // Required for std::swap

using namespace std;

// Function to generate all permutations
void generatePermutations(vector<string>& result, string current, int start) {
    // Base Case: If the current string length equals the original length,
    // we have generated a complete permutation.
    if (start == current.length()) {
        result.push_back(current);
        return;
    }

    // Iterate through the string starting from 'start'
    for (int i = start; i < current.length(); i++) {
        // Swap the current character with the start character
        swap(current[start], current[i]);

        // Recursively generate permutations for the remaining characters
        generatePermutations(result, current, start + 1);

        // Backtrack: Swap them back to restore the original order 
        // for the next iteration of the loop
        swap(current[start], current[i]);
    }
}

// Wrapper function to initiate the process
vector<string> permute(string s) {
    vector<string> result;
    generatePermutations(result, s, 0);
    return result;
}

// Driver code to test the function
int main() {
    string input = "ABC";

    vector<string> permutations = permute(input);

    cout << "All permutations of \"" << input << "\":" << endl;
    for (const string& p : permutations) {
        cout << p << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### How it Works:

1.  **Parameters:** The helper function `generatePermutations` takes the current state of the string (`current`), a result vector to store outputs, and an integer `start` representing the index we are currently filling.
2.  **Base Case:** If `start` is equal to the length of the string, we have successfully placed all characters, so we add the string to the result vector.
3.  **The Loop:** We loop from the `start` index to the end of the string.
4.  **Swap:** We swap the character at the `start` index with the character at the current loop index `i`. This brings a specific character to the "current" position.
5.  **Recurse:** We call the function recursively with `start + 1`. This effectively fixes the character at position `start` and generates all permutations for the rest of the string.
6.  **Backtrack:** After the recursive call returns, we swap the characters back. This undoes the change, allowing the loop to pick the next character for that specific position without interference from previous steps.

